Welsh legislators fear shame (AM)
The suggestion that the designation of Welsh legislators should be changed from AM (Assembly Member) to Member of the Welsh Parliament (MWP) has led to widespread confusion among some members.
A plan to change the name of the Welsh Parliament is the starting point.
Lawmakers from all political parties are concerned that this could lead to a ridiculous situation.
A member of the Labour Party is said to be concerned within the party that he will step on the rhyme of "Twp" and "Pwp."
For non-Welsh readers, Twp in Welsh means "smug" and Pwp means "food."
A member of the Pride Kammry Party said he proposed an alternative, saying the whole party was "dissatisfied."
A member of the Welsh Conservative Party also said that although it was "open" in response to the name change, there was not much difference in the pronunciation of MWP and "muppet".
By the way, the pronunciation of "w" in Welsh is similar to the English "u" with a Yorkshire accent.
A member committee currently drafting a name change said: "The final decision on the designation of the members of Congress is, of course, a matter entrusted to the members themselves."
According to the Welsh Governing Law, the Welsh Parliament was empowered to change its name.
In June, the committee released the results of a civil consultation on the proposal, which became clear that there was great support for the name change of Congress from Assembly to the Welsh Parliament.
With regard to the designation of lawmakers, the committee endorsed the Welsh Parliamer (WMP), but the MWP's proposal garnered the greatest support in the Citizen's talks.
Lawmakers are actually proposing alternatives, but the path to reaching an agreement could be a headache for Chairman Erin-Jones, who is required to submit a legislative draft on the name change within a couple of weeks.
The reform bill will also include other reforms related to the administration of Congress, including the rules on no-confidence decisions of legislators and the structure of the committee system.
In the course of deliberating on the bill, legislators will have the final power to make a final decision on what they should do with their names.
Macedonia holds referendum on country name change
A vote on whether to change its name to the Republic of North Macedonia will take place on Sunday.
The referendum was organized to resolve a decades-long dispute with neighboring Greece, which has a region called Macedonia within the country.
For many years, Greece has argued that the names of its northern neighbours are worthy of claims of ownership over Greek territory, and have firmly opposed Macedonia’s accession to the EU and NATO.
Macedonia's President Jorge Ivanov, who opposes a referendum on the country's name change, said he intended to ignore the results of the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, argue that the name change is the price of joining the EU and NATO.
