The Macedonians hold a referendum on changing the name of the country.
The Macedonians will either change the name of the country to the Republic of North Macedonia on Sunday or hold a referendum to resolve a decade-long dispute between the European Union and Greece, which had blocked its candidacy for NATO.
Greece, which has a state called Macedonia, voted to join NATO and the EU, saying that the people of the north are claiming against its territory.
The two governments signed an agreement on the newly proposed name in June, but opponents of nationalism argue that the change would destroy the national identity of Macedonia’s major Slavic population.
President Georgi Ivanov does not vote for a referendum, and a boycott of whether the voter turnout will meet a minimum of fifty per cent.
“Do you agree to accept an agreement with Greece and become a member of NATO and the EU?”
Proponents of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, have argued that it is worthwhile for Macedonia, one of the countries that have emerged since the collapse of Yugoslavia, to join institutions such as the EU and NATO.
"I'm going to be able to live freely under the umbrella of the European Union because I've come to vote today for Macedonia's young people and for the future of the country, because it means a safer life for all of us," Skopje's Olivera Georgijevska, 79-year-old said.
He said it was not legally binding, but that a sufficient number of members of Congress would comply with the outcome of the vote for the decision.
A name change would require two-thirds of the parliamentary majority.
The week's election commissioner said there had been no reports of any wrongdoing by 1 pm.
Sixty-six percent of registered voters, however, received only sixteen per cent of the vote, compared to thirty-four per cent in the last congressional vote.
"I came to vote for my children, our place is in Europe," Gjose Tanevski, a 62-year-old voter in Skopje, said.
Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, his wife Zorica and his son Dasco, took part in a Macedonian referendum on the name change of the country, which would pave the way to join NATO and the European Union in Stormica, Macedonia, on September 30, 2018.
In front of Skopje's parliament, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54-year-old pulled a chair in front of a tent installed by people preparing for a small stage and boycotting the referendum.
"We are in favor of NATO and the EU, but we want to participate with caution, not through the service doors," Kavadarkov said.
"We're a poor country, but we're dignified.
If they don't accept us as Macedonia, we can rely on other countries like China and Russia to become a part of European-Asian integration."
Prime Minister Sayev said NATO membership would bring the necessary investment to Macedonia, where the unemployment rate is more than twenty percent.
"More than 80 per cent of the people support the EU and NATO, so I believe that the vast majority will support it," Zaev said after the vote.
He said the "yes" result would be "proof of our future."
According to public opinion released last Monday, according to the Macedonian Policy Research Institute, thirty to forty-three percent of voters will participate in the referendum – below the necessary voter turnout.
In another public opinion conducted by Macedonia's Thelma TV, 57 per cent of respondents said they planned to go to the polls on Sunday.
Seventy per cent of them said they would vote for consent.
It takes 50 per cent and one vote for a successful referendum.
The failure of the referendum would be the first serious blow to pro-Western governments since taking over in May last year.
