"The sea belongs to us": Inland Bolivia wants the courts to reopen the way to the Pacific
The sailors patrol the naval headquarters equipped with La Paz's instruments.
The blue flags of the sea flutter in a public building.
The naval base from Lake Tichikaca to the Amazon is covered in its motto: "The sea belongs to us by rights.
It's my duty to get it back."
The memory of the coastline lost in Chile in the bloody resource conflict of the 19th century, through inland Bolivia, is still vivid - the longing to sail the Pacific Ocean again.
On October 1st, Bolivia, after five years of deliberations, waits for the decision of the International Court of Justice, so these hopes are perhaps the best of decades.
“Bolivia has momentum, also a spirit of unity and calmness, and, of course, we look forward to the results with a positive view,” said Bolivian diplomat Roberto Karzadhara.
Many Bolivians, looking at ICJ's ruling on a large screen across the country, optimistically, I expect the Hague court to agree with Bolivia's argument that, after decades of proper talks, Chile has an obligation to negotiate to give Bolivia an exit of sovereignty to the sea.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic indigenous president who is facing controversy in next year's re-election, will also take part in Monday's sentencing.
"It's so close that we're coming back to the Pacific," he vowed at the end of August.
However, some analysts believe that it is unlikely that the court will decide in Bolivia’s favor, and if that is the case, they won’t change much.
The Dutch-based U.N. agency does not have the power to award Chilean territory and does not determine the possible outcome of the talks.
The ICJ's ruling took place just six months after the final debate, indicating that the incident was "not complicated," said Paz Zarate, a Chilean expert in international law.
And Bolivia’s factors are far away, and in the last four years it may have retreated.
"The issue of access to the sea has been taken over by the current Bolivian government," Zarate said.
Morales' belligious rhetoric shattered Chile's goodwill that remained, she suggested.
Bolivia and Chile will continue to talk until a certain point, but it will be extremely difficult to continue discussions after this.
Since 1962, the two countries haven't exchanged ambassadors.
Eduardo Rodriguez Werze, former head of Bolivia in The Hague, denied the idea that the court's decision-making was unusually expeditious.
Bolivia on Monday will be a "special opportunity to open up a new era of relations with Chile" and an opportunity to "put an end to 139 years of disagreements on mutual benefits," he said.
Karzadira also denied that Morales (still one of Latin America's most popular presidents) was using maritime issues as political support.
"Bolivia will never give up access to the Pacific," he added.
“The verdict is an opportunity to know that we need to get through the past.”
If North Korea does not trust the U.S., nuclear disarmament will not happen.
