John Edward: Language skills essential for global citizens
Private schools in Scotland maintain an excellent academic performance, and this is another set of excellent test results that are enhanced only by individual and collective success in the achievements of sports, arts, music, and other communities.
These schools, represented by the Scottish Independent Schools Council (SCIS), with more than 30,000 pupils across Scotland, strive to provide the highest level of service to students and parents.
Private schools aim to ensure that students are prepared for higher education, their chosen careers, and living in society as global citizens.
In this educational sector, which allows you to design and implement custom-made school curriculums, you can see that modern languages continue to be the favorite subjects of the school.
Nelson Mandela said, "When you speak in words that you can understand, it goes into people's heads.
If you speak in his native language, it goes into that person's heart."
This reminds us not to rely solely on English when building relationships and trust with people in other countries.
This year’s recent exam results show that the language occupies the top of the league table, with the highest pass rate for students at private schools.
A total of 68 per cent of students studying a foreign language acquired a Higher Grade A.
In data collected from 74 SCIS-affiliated schools, 72 per cent of students studying Mandarin acquired Higher Grade A, 72 per cent of students learning German, 69 per cent of students learning French, and 63 per cent from learning Spanish have also acquired A.
This shows that private schools in Scotland are encouraging foreign language learning as an important skill that will definitely be needed for children and young people in the future.
Currently, the language is treated as an elective in the curriculum of a private school, just like STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics).
Seventy-seven percent of the reasons employers struggled to fill vacancies were due to a lack of language skills.
As a result, language skills have become increasingly essential for young people to prepare for their future careers.
In a situation where there are more job opportunities that require language due to globalization, these skills are essential.
No matter what kind of career you choose, if you have acquired a foreign language, you have acquired such lifelong skills, and it will be really advantageous in the future.
Being able to communicate directly with people from foreign countries means that multilingual human resources have a competitive advantage.
According to YouGov's survey of more than 4,000 British adults in 2013, 75 per cent don't have enough foreign language ability to have a conversation, and the ratio of people who can speak is 15 per cent in French.
Investing in language education is important for today’s children.
Especially for children in developing countries, having multiple language skills will increase their chances of getting a meaningful job.
In Scotland, different languages are taught by different schools.
While many schools will focus on more traditional modern languages, other schools will be teaching languages that are considered to be the UK’s most important, looking forward to 2020, such as Mandarin and Japanese.
No matter what your child is interested in, there are plenty of language options in private schools with specialized teachers in this field.
Private schools in Scotland are focused on providing a learning environment where children, whatever the future, have the skills they need to prepare for the future and develop the skills they need to succeed.
In a global business environment, languages continue to be crucial to the country’s future, so it needs to be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should be regarded as “international communication skills.”
Private schools will continue to provide young people in Scotland with this choice, diversity, and even better education.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is a member of the Board of Trustees of the Council of Private Schools in Scotland.
