Trump's West Virginia rallies, YouTube channels
President Donald Trump will launch a series of election rallies tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
This is the first of five gatherings scheduled for next week, including places friendly to Trump, such as Tennessee and Mississippi.
With Supreme Court justices on hold, Trump aims to solidify support for the upcoming midterm elections as Republicans risk losing their parliamentary majority in November’s elections.
At what time will Trump's rally in West Virginia take place tonight, and how can I watch it online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for Saturday, September 29, 2018, at 7pm (Eastern Standard Time).
Trump's rallies in West Virginia can be viewed online on a live stream on YouTube below.
Trump is likely to comment on this week's Kavanaugh hearings amid a tense situation, with the Senate's appropriation vote postponed for up to a week while the FBI investigates the alleged sexual assault of Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
However, the main purpose of this continuous rally is to give momentum to the momentum of the Republican Party, which is facing a difficult climate for November’s elections.
According to Reuters, President Trump’s five rallies next week are aimed at “strengthening volunteers and supporters to protect and expand the majority of Republican seats in the Senate and House.”
"Securing a majority of parliamentary seats is very important for his policies, so the president plans to visit as many states as possible for a hectic election season," a Trump campaign spokesman who wished to remain anonymous told Reuters.
Tonight's rallies scheduled at Wheeling's Wesbanco arena, according to West Virginia's Metro News, said: "It could attract supporters from Ohio and Pennsylvania, which could be reported in Pittsburgh media.
Trump won more than 40 per cent in West Virginia in 2016, but Saturday's visit to West Virginia would be his second visit in a month by Trump.
Trump is trying to support Patrick Morrissey, the Republican candidate for West Virginia, who lags behind in the polls.
According to Reuters, Simon Hader, a political scientist at the University of West Virginia, said: "It's not a good sign for Morrissey that the president is coming to boost him."
