North Korea says it will 'absolutely' 'absolutely' to disarm unilaterally without trust
North Korea's foreign minister told the U.N. on Saturday that the ensuing sanctions had led to growing distrust of the U.S. and would not unilaterally abandon nuclear weapons under such circumstances.
Lee Yong-ho said at the U.N. annual meeting that North Korea has taken "significant well-intention measures" over the past year, including the suspension of nuclear tests and missile tests, the dismantling of nuclear testing sites, and a pledge not to spread nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
"But we don't see a substantial response from the U.S.," he said.
“If we don’t trust the U.S., trust in our national security will be lost, and we won’t unilaterally disarm under those circumstances.”
Lee reiterated North Korea’s dissatisfaction that the U.S. government does not reward North Korea’s “gradual” denuclearization approach, but his statement is, unlike in the past, the North Korean government’s failure to immediately reject unilateral denuclearization.
At the first summit between the U.S. president and North Korean leaders in Singapore on June 12th, Foreign Minister Lee said Kim Jong-un had pledged to work towards "denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula," as well as a joint statement in which Trump pledged North Korea's security.
North Korea calls for a formal end to the 1950-53 Korean War, but the U.S. says North Korea must first give up its nuclear weapons.
The U.S. government has also resisted calls to ease harsh international sanctions on North Korea.
“The U.S. insists on ‘denuclearization first,’ and in order to compulsively achieve its objectives, we are applying further pressure through sanctions and opposing the ‘declaration of the end of the war,’” Li said.
“The recognition that sanctions can succumb to us is an illusion of people who don’t know us.
The problem, however, is that the continuation of the sanctions reinforces our disbelief.”
Foreign Minister Lee did not mention Kim and Trump's plans for a second summit, which President Trump emphasized at the United Nations earlier this week.
Instead, he highlighted the past five months of Kim's talks with South Korean President Moon Jae-in, adding: "If South Korea were a party to denuclearization rather than the U.S., the denuclearization of the Korean peninsula wouldn't have come to an end."
Even so, the tone of Lee’s speech is dramatically different from the time when Kim called Trump an “Evil President” and stated at last year’s U.S. General Assembly that North Korea’s rockets made it inevitable to reach the U.S. mainland, after Trump called him a “Rotke Man” in a suicide operation.
Trump, who threatened to "completely destroy" North Korea last year, praised gold at the U.N. this year for his courage over its efforts to disarm, but said there were many challenges and that sanctions need to be maintained until North Korea denuclearizes.
On Wednesday, Trump said, "It doesn't matter if it's two or three years, it doesn't matter if it's five months," and he didn't set this time frame.
China and Russia have objected that the U.N. Security Council should reward North Korea’s actions.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: "The Security Council sanctions must remain severe until we verify that complete and ultimately denuclearization took place."
The Security Council has unanimously tightened its sanctions on North Korea since 2006 in order to cut off funds for North Korea's nuclear and ballistic missile developments.
Pompeo said he had a meeting with Foreign Minister Li during the U.N. General Assembly, after which he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for the second summit.
Pompeo had visited North Korea three times earlier this year, but his last visit yielded no results.
After leaving Pyongyang in July, he said there had been progress, but within a few hours North Korea accused him of making "gang-like demands."
During a meeting with President Moon this month, North Korea pledged to dismantle missile and nuclear facilities if the U.S. takes "responding measures."
He said that the “responsible measures” that Kim is looking for is the security that Trump has promised in Singapore, a move towards the normalization of relations with the U.S. government.
Harvard students take courses to get plenty of rest
Harvard’s new course this year aims to help all undergraduates get more sleep in order to fight the expansion of the macho culture of taking caffeine and studying “all-night.”
The world’s greatest Harvard students are often ignorant about very basic things, such as how to take care of their bodies.
Charles Zeissler, a professor of sleep medicine at Harvard University's School of Medicine and a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, designed the course, which he believes is the first of its kind in the U.S.
He said he came up with the idea of starting the course after giving a talk on the effects of lack of sleep on learning.
"Finally, a female student came up to me and said, "Why do I only hear this story when I'm in fourth grade?"
I was surprised because no one told her about the importance of sleep," he told the Telegraph.
For the first time this year, the course will explain to students how good sleeping habits promote learning and exercise and improve general well-being.
Paul Bareira, a professor of psychiatry at Harvard School of Medicine and executive director of the university's medical services, says the university decided to introduce a course after recognizing the severe sleep deprivity among students that week.
A one-hour course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one section, students can learn about coffee cups, curtains, sneakers, and books in the dormitory room about the effects of caffeine and light, the effects of sleep-deprived exercise, and the importance of daily routines related to bedtime.
In a separate section, participants explained how long-term sleep deprivation can promote the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression and cancer.
In addition, on the map of the campus with interactive icons, participants are encouraged to think about their daily routines.
“We know that the behavior of students will not change immediately.
However, they should have the right to know, just as they have the right to know how the choice of smoking affects their health,” Zeisler added.
In addition, the culture of "all-night study" still exists today, and the problem of lack of sleep is expanding due to modern technology and increasing pressure on students.
Getting good enough sleep is a “secret weapon” for students battling stress, exhaustion, and anxiety, he says, preventing weight gain as the lack of sleep puts the brain in hunger mode and is always hungry.
Raymond Thor, a 19-year-old from California who studies chemistry and physical biology, helped design his course after taking Professor Zeisler's class at Harvard last year when he was a first-year student.
He woke up and wanted to expand the course to the entire campus.
The next step he wants is to get all graduate students to receive a similar learning program before they begin to study in a competitive place at Harvard.
Professor Zeissler recommends that students set alarms at bedtime as well as wake-up time, and pay attention to the effects of harmful "blue lights" that are issued from electronic screens and LED lighting, causing circadian rhythms and sleep problems.
