Glenn Middleton of the Rangers appealed for a penalty when he was involved with Jacobs, but Thomson also dismissed it here.
Almanac: The inventor of Geiger Counter
"Sunday Morning" a page of Almanac: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago today, the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, which would eventually become a device known as Geiger counter.
Geiger Counter, the mainstay of science since then, has also become the mainstay of pop culture, as seen in the 1950 film "Coronado's Bell" starring Roy Rogers and Dale Evans dressed as seemingly unlikely cowboy scientists.
Man: “What the hell is that?”
Rodgers: "It's a Geiger counter that's used to detect radioactive minerals like uranium.
If you connect the earphones, you can actually hear the effects of the atoms released by the radiation in the minerals."
Evans: "It's true. There's a sound to play!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
However, the invention bearing his name lives on.
The new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to 'determining' faulty cells
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “determine” and kill defective cells.
Vaccines teach immune systems to recognize faulty cells as part of treatment
In this way, immune cells are extracted from the patient and changes are made in the laboratory.
As a result, cells are able to “determine” the proteins common to many cancers and re-inject them.
The trial vaccine shows promising results among patients with a wide range of cancers.
In the case of women who received a vaccine that taught the immune system to recognize faulty cells, ovarian cancer disappeared for more than 18 months.
In this way, by extracting immune cells from the patient and making changes in the laboratory, it is now possible to “determine” the proteins common to many cancers called HER2, after which these cells are re-injected.
Jay Berzovski, a professor at the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland, said: "Our results showed the effectiveness of the vaccine."
HER2 promotes the growth of several types of cancer, including breast cancer, ovarian cancer, lung cancer, and colorectal cancer, Professor Berzovsky explains.
A similar approach of “teaching” how to extract immune cells from patients and target cancer cells is also effective in treating a kind of leukemia.
