Game of Thrones star Kit Harrington attacks poisoned masculinity
Kit Harrington is known as John Snow, a master of swords, in HBO's violent medieval fantasy series "Game of Thrones."
However, the thirty-one-year-old kit attacked the stereotypes of such macho heroes, saying that these roles on the screen have made the boys feel that they have to be strong in order to be respected by the people around them.
In a conversation with the Sunday Times culture, Kit said he felt “something was wrong” and asked how he could tackle the issue of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo era.
Kitt, who was recently married to Game of Thrones co-star Rose Leslie, said he felt “very strongly” working on the issue.
"I personally feel very strongly about what went wrong in terms of masculinity," he said.
“What have you taught the growing boys about the issues we’re looking at now?”
Kit believes that television may be part of the blame for the increase in toxic masculinity, thanks to a very masculine character.
He continues: "What's been born and what's been taught?
Don’t you feel that boys have to be men with certain qualities because of what they saw on TV and on the streets?
I think this is one of the big problems in this day and age, but how can we change that?
If you look at a young man, it's obvious that something is wrong."
In the interview, he also said, “Battlefields and horses are enough,” and admitted that Game of Thrones won’t talk about the prequel or sequel after next summer.
Starting in November, he will appear in Sam Sheppard's "True West," a re-enactment. This is the story of a film producer and his younger brother's styling of theft.
He recently said that the best thing about Game of Thrones was meeting his wife, Rose.
“I met my wife on this show, so that’s what he gave me my future life with my family in the future,” he said.
Rose played Jon Snow's lover Igritte, who plays Kit in this Emmy Award-winning fantasy series.
The two got married at Leslie's family home in Scotland in June 2018.
HIV/AIDS: China reports 14% surge in new cases
China announced a 14 percent increase in the number of people infected with HIV and AIDS.
More than 82 million people in the country are affected, health officials said.
About 4 million new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
Most of the new cases have been transmitted through sexual intercourse and have seen changes from the past.
Formerly in China, HIV spread rapidly in some areas as a result of a transfusion of infected blood.
However, the number of people infected with HIV in this way has fallen to almost zero, Chinese health officials said at a meeting in Yunnan.
However, the number of people infected with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by one hundred thousand year-on-year.
In China’s LGBT community, HIV transmission by sexual intercourse has become a serious problem.
In China, homosexuality was legalized in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is said to be pervasive.
According to the study, due to the country’s conservative values, 70 to 90 percent of male homosexuals will eventually marry women.
Most infections occur due to deficiencies in preventive measures in these relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged to make anti-HIV drugs more widely accessible as part of its efforts to address the issue.
Maxine Waters Denies Staff's Data Leakage of Republican Senters, Accusing "Dangerous Lies" and "Conspiracy Theories"
U.S. Rep. Maxine Waters, a U.S. congressman, denied the accusations on Saturday that one of her staff had posted the personal information of three Republican senators on the lawmaker's Wikipedia page.
Los Angeles Democrats have asserted that the allegations are being made by "ultra-wright" critics and websites.
"Lies, lies, despicable lies," Waters said in a Twitter statement.
The leaked information reportedly included the home addresses and phone numbers of Senator Lindsey Graham from South Carolina and Senators Mike Lee and Olyn Hatch from Utah.
This information was posted online on Thursday by an anonymous figure on Capitol Hill in the midst of a Senate committee hearing about Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh's alleged sexual assault.
The data breach occurred after three senators interrogated Kavanaugh.
Conservative sites such as Gateway Pandit and RedState have said their IP addresses belong to Water's offices and have released information about Water's employees, The Hill reported.
"This unsubstantiated allegations are completely false, and absolutely false," Waters continued.
“My staff has compromised their identity, personal information, and safety due to these fraudulent and false allegations, but they are not involved in the leakage of this information at all.
This unsubstantiated claim is a complete falsehood, and it is an absolute lie."
Water's statement was immediately criticised online by people, including former White House press secretary Ali Fleischer.
"This denial is nothing but anger," Fleischer wrote.
“This shows that she’s not worthy of Congress.
If you are accused of doing something you have not done, do not be angry.
Don’t be arrogant.
Don't ask about the defendant's motives.
You have to behave calmly and peacefully."
Fleischer appears to be comparing Waters' reaction to Democrats' criticisms that he was too angry about Justice Kavano's attitude at Thursday's hearing.
Omar Navarro, a Republican candidate who is aiming for Waters' seat in the midterm elections, also expressed his opinion on Twitter.
"It's amazing if it's true," he muttered.
In a statement, Waters said her office had “contacted jurisdictional authorities and the police with these false claims.”
She said, "We will do everything in our power to ensure that the perpetrators are identified," she said. "We will also hold my staff legally liable for a destructive and dangerous act."
"Johnny English: Analog's Counterattack" Movie Review - Spiage Parody of Incomprehensity
Recently, it has been decided to find the meaning of Brexit in a new film depicting Britain’s decline, but that seems to be true of Johnny English’s sequel action comedies, and this series has so far released “Johnny English” and “Johnny English Reward”.
Could this irony of self-satisfaction with the theme of how incompetent we have become an opportunity for Britain's new exports?
In any case, Johnny English, whose eyes pop out and has a rubber-like incompetent face, has also repeated blunders in this work. His name, above all, suggests that he is a comedy made for movie lovers outside the English-speaking world.
Of course, he’s a stupid secret agent who misunderstands that he has sophisticated charm, but he has all the elements of Captain Cruzo, Mr. Bean, and that character who played only one sound of the runner’s theme song of fire at the opening ceremony of the London Olympics.
Johnny English was originally based on a mysterious world traveler in trouble, played by Atkinson in a now-forgotten Barclay Card television advert.
There is one or two good scenes in this latest project.
When Johnny English approaches a helicopter wearing a medieval armor, the rotor blade hits the helmet and makes a noise.
Atkinson’s talented comedy has also been demonstrated, but especially given that “serious” films such as 007 and Mission Impossible have come to include comedy elements, the humour is quite incomprehensive and strangely unnecessary.
Its humour feels like it’s for children, not adults, and for me Johnny English’s quirky adventures aren’t as inventive as the gags of a Silent movie at Mr Bean played by Atkinson, and they don’t seem to have the same focus.
The film presupposes that the UK is currently facing a serious problem.
When cyberhackers infiltrate a web network of Britain’s top-secret espionage and expose the identities of all active British agents, their agents (unfortunately small roles for Kevin Elton) get upset.
This news was beyond the limit for an arrogant prime minister who already had a lot of problems and had a declining approval rating.Emma Thompson has played the parody of Theresa May well, but there is no depth in the script.
Her smart adviser said she had no choice but to convene a retired spie because the identities of all active-duty spies had been made public.
For this reason, Johnny English, who works as the headmaster of an elegant school and teaches him how to become a covert operative while saying that it is a secret, has also been approached, and some interesting gags can be seen at the English spy academy such as the School of Rock.
English rushes to the Whitehole for a briefing and reunites with his former partner Boff (Ben Miller), who has been struggling for many years.
Boff is now married to the commander of the submarine (a cheerful, hot-blooded character; a waste to Vicky Pepardine) and is married.
Then, Her Majesty's Secret Service's Otoboke Combi is revived, and he meets the beautiful Fem Fatal, Ophelia Britova, played by Olga Kurylenko.
On the other hand, the prime minister is still with the charismatic billionaire Jason Volta (Jake Lacey), who claims to solve Britain’s computer’s predicament.
English and Boff create a number of troubles as they used to, posing as a waiter, starting a fire at a luxurious French restaurant, making a fuss about breaking into Volta’s lavish yacht, and disrupting English’s attempts to use a virtual reality headset to get to know Volta’s house.
It is true that all the gimmicks are gathered in the last mountain, but this beloved slapstick comedy seems to be generally a children's program.
It's a pretty ordinary thing.
And, as I felt in other Johnny English films, I couldn’t help but think that the British film industry couldn’t give Rowan Atkinson a role worthy of his talent.
The Labour Party denies considering a five-day payout plan for working four days a week for its people.
Jeremy Corbyn’s Labour Party is expected to consider a radical plan in which citizens will be paid five days’ worth of work four days a week.
The party says the company’s executives want to share the money they get through the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution with their workers by increasing their salaries.
Under the plan, employees are expected to work on a three-day work week with the same salary.
Sources said the idea was in line with the Party's economic policies and plans to make it a country that would benefit workers.
The shift to a four-day-week system has also been endorsed by the trade union assembly as a way for workers to benefit from the changing economy.
Speaking to the Sunday Times, a senior Labour official said: "A review of the policy is expected to be announced by the end of the year.
“It’s not something that can be done overnight, but the four-day working week is a goal that fits the Party’s approach to recalibrating the economy in favor of workers and the party’s overall industrial strategy.”
As the Greens have pledged a four-day labor system a week during the 2017 general election campaign, the Labour Party isn't supporting the idea for the first time.
At the moment, however, the Labour Party as a whole does not support that goal.
A Labour spokesman said: "The four-day-week labor scheme isn't the party's policy and the party doesn't consider it."
Shadow Minister John McDonnell embodied his vision for the socialist revolution in the economy at last week's labour conference.
McDonnell said he was determined to take back power from "faceless officers" and "unfortunated gainers" of utility companies.
The shadow minister’s plan also implies that the Labour government could “deduct” it because it was aware of wrongdoing, and that the current shareholders of the water company might not be able to take back their entire equity.
He also confirmed plans to put workers on the company's board of directors, create a comprehensive ownership fund to deposit 10 percent of the capital of the private sector there, and employees would receive annual dividends of up to £500.
Lindsey Gram and John Kennedy talk about whether the FBI could change their minds in an investigation into Kavanaugh in '60 Minutes'
The FBI’s investigation into Justice Brett Kavanaugh’s indictment delayed his final vote by at least a week on nominating the Supreme Court, raising the question of whether the agency’s findings could jeopardize the support of Republican senators.
