Then, after the accusation, I called him and said to him, “Did you do it?”
He was resolutely determined and obvious."
However, Gram's vote seems unchangeable.
"When it comes to Brett Kavaneau, I've made up my mind and it's going to be heavily condemned," he said.
"Dr. Ford, I don't know what happened, but I understand this: Brett violently denied it," Gram added, referring to Christine Brazyford.
"And no one who gave her name could verify it.
It’s been thirty-six years.
I don't see any new changes."
What is the Global Citizen’s Festival and has it helped reduce poverty?
In New York this Saturday, an annual music event, the Global Citizen’s Festival will be held, where there are a lot of very impressive stars performing, but what is equally impressive is the mission to end the poverty of the world.
At this year's seven-year-old Global Citizen Festival, tens of thousands of people will not only enjoy performances such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes on the Lawn Square in Central Park, but will also raise awareness of the real goal of ending extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, which began in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international advocacy group that aspires to end poverty by increasing the number of people who are actively fighting.
In order to receive free tickets for the event (unless you want to buy a VIP ticket), the concert participants had to volunteer, email to world leaders, make phone calls, or otherwise complete a series of “actions” that would take some meaningful throw to raise their sense of purpose of ending poverty.
But with twelve more years to go, how far has the Global Citizen’s Festival achieved that goal?
Is the idea of persuading people to take action with the reward of a free concert, or is it just a sincere way, or another case of “crickism” that makes you feel like you’ve done something meaningful by signing or tweeting an online petition?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has recorded more than 1.9 million "actions" among its supporters and says it is pushing various goals.
These actions have led world leaders to announce commitments and policies equivalent to more than $370bn, which will affect the lives of more than two and a half million people by the 2030s.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and announcements resulting from its actions, of which at least $100 billion had already been paid or financed.
The group estimates that the secured funds have had a direct impact on about 6.49 million people around the world so far.
The main commitment is to "help children grow to the fullest," which includes a UK-based investor-performer partnership of power-to-performers. The organization received more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizen and pledged to provide $35 million to Rwanda to help eliminate malnutrition from the country.
"With the support of British governments, donors, governments, and global citizens like you, we can make the social injustice of malnutrition a past." During a live concert in London last April, Ambassador of Power of Nutrition, Tracy Ul-Man told the audience.
The group also said more than 5,000 actions in the UK calling for improved nutrition among mothers and children, and the government announced financial support for the Power of Nutrition, a project that delivers nutrition to five million women and children.
In response to the question in the FAQ on its website, “Do you think we can end extreme poverty?”
Global Citizen said, "It's going to be a long and difficult road, sometimes it's going to fail.
However, since we have the power to gather, we will succeed like the great civil rights and the anti-apartheid movement.
Janet Jackson, Weekend, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, and Janel Monae are among the performers at this year's New York event, hosted by Deborah-Lee Furnes and Hugh Jackman.
The U.S. can use the Navy for a "blockade" to prevent Russia's energy exports - Interior Secretary
The U.S. government can rely on the Navy in areas, including the Middle East, "if necessary" to prevent Russia's energy from hitting the market, U.S. Home Secretary Ryan Zink revealed, Washington Egzaminer reported.
Zinku argued that Russia's involvement in Syria was a pretext to open up new energy markets, especially when operating at the invitation of a legitimate government.
“The reason they’re in the Middle East is because they want to mediate their energy, just like in Eastern Europe and southern Europe,” he said.
According to officials, there are methods and means to tackle it.
"The U.S., together with the Navy, can make sure that the sea lanes are open and, if necessary, can be sealed off so that energy does not go to the market," he said.
Zink was speaking to attendees at an event organized by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-profit organization that builds its position as the "voice of energy consumers" in the United States.
He compared Washington's approach to dealing with Russia and Iran, noting that they are virtually the same.
“The economic option for Iran and Russia, more or less, is to replace it with fuel,” he said, referring to Russia as an “one-tricky pony” that is economically dependent on fossil fuels.
The Trump administration is on a mission to promote the export of liquefied natural gas to Europe as a much cheaper option for European consumers on behalf of Russia.
To that end, Trump administration officials, including President Trump himself, are trying to convince Germany to get out of the “inappropriate” Nord Stream2 pipeline plan.
Moscow reiterated that the $110 billion Nord Stream 2 pipeline, which plans to double its existing pipeline capacity to 1,100 billion cubic metres, is a purely economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington's strong opposition to the project is simply due to economic reasons and is an example of unfair competition.
“I believe we share the idea that energy can’t be a tool for pressure, and that consumers should be able to choose suppliers.”
The U.S. stance provoked a backlash from Germany, and Germany reaffirmed its commitment to the project.
Germany's main industrial group, the German Industrial Federation (BDI), called on the U.S. not to speak out on EU energy policies and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
"It's a big problem for a third country to interfere with our energy supply," Dieter Kemp, president of the German Confederation of Industry (BDI), said following a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
