U.S. Ambassador Accuses of 'bullying' using China's 'propagastic ad
A week after China's official newspaper published a four-page ad promoting the mutual benefits of U.S.-China trade, China's U.S. ambassador denounced Beijing for using U.S. newspapers for propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump denied China last Wednesday, but accused China of trying to interfere in U.S. congressional elections on November 6th, and then referred to a paid advertisement for the China Dilly newspaper in Iowa's largest newspaper, the Demoyne Register.
Trump’s accusation that Beijing was trying to interfere in the U.S. election, as U.S. officials told Reuters, has shown that it is a new phase of a campaign that puts more and more pressure on China by Washington.
Trade-promoting advertising is normal for foreign governments, but Beijing and Washington are now in the midst of an expanding trade war that imposes the same-rate tariffs on each other’s imports.
China's retaliatory tariffs in the early days of the trade war were designed to hit exporters in states like Iowa, which supported Trump-led Republicans, Chinese and U.S. experts said.
Terry Branstad, China's U.S. ambassador to the United States and a long-time governor of Iowa, a major exporter of agricultural products to China in the past, said Beijing had hurt American workers, farmers and businesses.
China’s Branstad posted a statement on Sunday’s statements at the Dérþin Register, saying: “The invasion of propaganda ads against the freedom of the press is intensifying.”
According to Branstade, "the Chinese government is using America's important traditions of freedom of speech and the freedom of the press for its own benefit by posting paid public notices on the Demain Register in order to spread propaganda."
“In contrast, there is only a limited number of dissenting opinions here in Beijing, and you can’t really see the different opinions that the Chinese people would have about China’s turbulent economic trajectory, which is under the control of the Chinese Communist Party media,” he said.
"One of China's most prominent newspapers declined to publish my articles," he added, but he declined to specifically mention which newspaper.
Analysts warn that the uproars surrounding Mr. Kavaner will move away from the Republican Party before the midterm elections.
