The inventor of the World Wide Web plans to launch a new Internet to replace Google and Facebook.
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the Worldwide Web, launched his startup business to become a rival to Facebook, Amazon, and Google.
Inrupt is the latest project by Lee, a technology legend, and is based on Berners Lee’s open-source platform Solid.
Solid gives users the option of where their data is stored and who can access which information.
In an exclusive Fast interview, Berners-Lee joked that Inrupt's hidden intention was "world domination."
As for the start-up business, he said, “This is what we need to do right now.”
“This is a historic moment.”
The app uses Solid’s technology to create its own “Personal Online Data Store (POD)”.
This includes contact lists, to-do lists, calendars, music libraries and other personal and professional tools.
It can be used simultaneously from a single browser, such as Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack, and Spotify.
What’s unique about a personal online data store is that it’s entirely up to the user who has access to what information.
Inrupt calls it “individual empowerment through data.”
Inrupt’s CEO, John Bruce, said Inrupt’s idea is to provide resources, processes, and the right skills for everyone to use.
Inrupt currently consists of a security platform acquired by Berners-Lee, Bruce and IBM, on-staff developers who have signed up for the project, and a community of volunteer coders.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world will be able to create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on Inrupt’s website.
Berners Lee said he and his team are “talking about introducing a complete change that will make Facebook and Google’s business model overnight.”
“I don’t ask them for permission.”
In Saturday's media release, Berners-Lee said of Inrupt: "The mission is to provide a commercial energy and ecosystem that guarantees the integrity and quality of the new web that will be built on Solid."
Berners-Lee founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1994, revolutionizing the Internet.
Over the past few months, Berners Lee has continued to make influential statements in a discussion of net neutrality.
Berners-Lee retains his position as founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, Web Foundation, and Open Data Institute, even during the launch of Inrupt.
"I'm very optimistic about this next-generation web," Berners Lee added.
Bernard Van: World War World War World Crucifixion: Celebration to honor clerics
As a combatant, a celebration was held in his home town for one hundred years in relation to the clergy of the Church of England, which won the Order of the Victorian Cross during World War I.
Reverend Lt. Col. Bernard Van won the Medal on September 29, 1918, due to the Battle of the Bergries and the roucourt.
Four days later, however, he was killed by a sniper, and he did not know that he was honoured with the highest honour of the British Army.
On Saturday, a parade was held in Lashden, Northampshire, and a commemorative stone was opened to the public by his two grandchildren.
One of his grandsons, Michael Van, said that the stone monument was "a tremendously symbolic thing" and that just a hundred years after the feat of my grandfather being given a medal, it was just one hundred years later.
According to the London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant Colonel Van led a platoon through Canal de Saint-Kantan through a "very thick fog and a fierce fire from the front and machine guns."
After that, with "great courage" at the forefront of the fire, he rushed with a small gun in one hand, pushed back the front line, and repulsed the throttle.
On October 4th, Captain Van was killed by a German sniper, just about a month before the end of the war.
Seventy-two-year-old Michael Van said of his grandfather’s actions: “It’s something I can’t imitate, but it’s humiliating.”
His brother, Dr. James Vann, gave a grand celebration after a parade led by the Royal Brentwood Youth Band.
"It was a tremendous honour to be part of the parade," Michael Van said, adding that "the courage of a true hero has been demonstrated with the support of many people."
Only Peppa Pigg, an MMA fan who stayed up all night trying to see the Beratol 206, could be seen.
I want you to imagine how I felt when I didn't see this main event, even though I stayed up all night because I wanted to see Beratol206.
The building of San Jose consisted of thirteen matches, of which six of the main cards were aired throughout the night in Chennell5 in the United Kingdom.
At 6 a.m., while Gegar Musasi and Rory Macdonald were preparing for the match, the broadcast switched to Peppa Pig, which also caught British viewers.
Viewers who stayed up without sleeping until early in the morning for this match were particularly disappointed.
One fan wrote on Twitter that it was "some kind of bad joke" about being replaced by a children's show.
Asked about the broadcast, Verator’s senior vice-president of marketing and telecommunications, Dave Schwartz said, “At six in the morning, the government’s regulation that martial arts is not suitable for viewing has forced us to switch to children’s programming.”
"Peppa pig is suitable."
Scott Kocker, president of Beratol, said he would consider adjusting the schedule, taking into account British viewers in the future.
