However, it was six o’clock on Sunday morning, so I couldn’t adjust it this Sunday, by Monday’s broadcast time in the United Kingdom.
We're in the process of adjusting right now.
There were a lot of texts coming and going when the broadcast was switched. All of the contents were dissatisfied.
I made an effort to bring it back. I thought there was a technical flaw.
But it wasn't, and it's a matter related to the government.
You can promise that nothing like this will happen in the next broadcast.
I will try to reduce it from six fights to the usual five. I tried to provide as many games as possible for the fans, but it turned out.
This was an unfortunate situation."
Desert Island Disc: Tom Dalay felt a sense of inferiority for his sexuality
Tom Darley, an Olympic diver, grew up feeling inferior to others in terms of sexuality, but that motivated him to succeed.
Twenty-four-year-old Dale didn’t realize he was “different from the kids around him” until he went to middle school.
At the Radio4 Desert Island disc, where Lauren Raver is a presenter, Darre talked about gay rights in order to give "hope" to others.
He also said that becoming a parent made him less interested in winning the Olympics.
Kirsty Young, a regular presenter for this long-running TV show, had not appeared for several months due to illness.
Appeared as a castaway on Mr. Rabern's first show, Darley said he felt that he was "underperformed" by everyone, because "it wasn't socially acceptable to like both boys and girls."
He said: “To this day, feelings of inferiority and alienation have always been real, giving us strength and strength for success.”
He said he wanted to prove to himself that he had "some kind of value." He said that he wouldn't disappoint anyone when it becomes clear that he's a sexuality one day.
Dale, who won two bronze medals at the Olympics, has become a hugely influential LGBT campaign participant. He appeared at the Commonwealth Games held in Australia this year and appealed to more countries to legalize homosexuality.
Dale said he was lucky enough to be able to live without hindrance while opening up his sexuality, and he decided to take the plunge and speak to others to give them “hope.”
The three-time world champion said he fell in love with American filmmaker Dustin Reims Black. He said he was “surprised” when he met him last year.
Dalley is married to Mr. Black, who once won an Oscar for him twenty years old. However, the age gap is not a problem at all.
“I’ve had a lot of experiences since I was very young,” he said, adding that he participated in the Olympics for the first time at the age of fourteen. Three years later, he lost his father to cancer. It was difficult to find an experienced opponent at the same age who had lived such ups and downs.
The couple became parents in June. Their son was Robert Ray Black-Dalley. The birth of their son changed “all values,” he said.
“If I had been asked about this last year, it would have been all about getting a gold medal,” he said.
“In fact, it’s more important than an Olympic gold medal.
For me, the Olympic gold medal is the lobby."
His son was diagnosed with a brain tumor and took over the name of his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40.
Dulay says his father couldn’t accept death. One of the last words he left behind was that he had already arranged tickets for the London Games. His father said he wanted to watch it in the front row.
“By the time I watched in the front row, I couldn’t say that I wasn’t in this world anymore.”
“I was holding my hand until my father’s breathing stopped. When I actually stopped breathing and my father died, I realized for the first time that my father wasn’n’t invincible,” he says.
The following year, Dulay took part in the 2012 Olympics and won a bronze medal.
"At that time, I thought this was everything I wanted in my life, and I didn't think there would be a more pleasant feeling than a dive in front of the home audience at the Olympic Games," he said.
Also, this experience was an inspiration that led to Dale’s first selection. Heather Small’s “Proud” resonates with the process of growth on his way to the Olympics. Listening to this song still gives me goosebumps, he said.
Desert Island Disc, BBC Radio 4, Sunday UK time 11:15
Mickelson is benched in Saturday's Ryder Cup
America's Phil Mickelson is trying to set a new record in Sunday's 47th Ryder Cup, but he needs to re-examine the form so that this doesn't turn into an unfortunate outcome.
Mickelson, who is trying to set a record of twelve appearances at the event once every two years, was benched by captain Jim Furik for Saturday’s Fourballs and Foursome.
Mickelson, who has won five major championships, has attracted attention at many tournaments in the United States. This time, he will participate in the competition while cheering away from that role. The aim is to eliminate the cause of the disorder.
It's the pinnacle of his career right now, but the driver isn't perfectly linear. The 48-year-old Mickelson isn't in the ideal condition to take on the Le Golf National Course. On this track, Rongraf leads to a misshot.
The course itself is grueling, but in Sunday's ninth game, he will face Francesco Molinari, the winner of the British Open. Molinari has teamed up with rookie Tommy Fleetwood to win all four games this week.
If the U.S. starts twelve single matches with four points lost, it will be a heated match from the beginning, and Mickelson’s game will be exactly decisive.
Furik said he trusted Mickelson, but he didn't say much else.
"He's well-understood what he's going to do today, slapping my back lightly, putting my shoulders together and saying he's ready for tomorrow," Furik said.
“There seems to be enough confidence.
He's a player who's been inducted into the Hall of Fame, and he's made a huge contribution to the team in the past, and that hasn't changed this week.
I didn’t expect him to play two games.
I thought I was going to play more, but I made adjustments like this, as a result of thinking that this was a necessary measure.
I want to participate more, just like everyone else thinks."
Mickelson surpassed Nick Faldo's record on Sunday, setting the record for the most Ryder Cup appearances.
As a result, Mickelson's career in the Ryder Cup could come to an end, and his achievements so far have never matched his personal record.
Mickelson's track record is eighteen wins, twenty losses and seven draws. However, Furik says that his presence has brought a spiritual basis to the team.
"He's funny, it's cynical, and he likes to mess with people. I'd be grateful to be in the team room."
"I'm sure the young players are enjoying working with him this week. It's interesting to watch.
He brings us more than a play."
European captain Thomas Bjorn realizes that the big lead will soon disappear
From his own experience, European captain Thomas Bjorn has shown that even if he leads big at the start of the Ryder Cup single on the final day, the situation can change.
Born in Denmark made his debut for Balderama in 1997. At the time, a team led by Sebe Balesteros had a five-point lead over the U.S., but as a result he managed to win with a slight difference of 141⁄2-131⁄2.
Mr. Beyon in the photo is Le Golf National, after watching the 2018 team win 5-3 on Friday, and after watching him lead 10-6 yesterday, "Remember that he was also taking a big lead in the Barderama. I took a big lead at Brookline, but I lost at that time. Even in Balderama, I had a big lead, but I won at the last minute.
This kind of history shows me and the team that it has not been settled yet.
So I’ll do my best tomorrow.
You just have to come and do what needs to be done.
It's not the end of the game until the score appears.
We have a goal. It's about winning trophies. We're going to do everything we can towards this goal.
That’s what I’ve been saying for a long time, and I’m passionate about the twelve players on my team. But I know what kind of players they’re. They’re the strongest players in the world.”
Expressing his delight that the players of my team are playing an active role on a difficult golf course, Bjorn added: "I try to never be arrogant.
There will be enemies tomorrow.
Tomorrow's individual performance will be a highlight, and there will be a different element.
It’s great to be able to be with your friends when it’s going well, but in individual competitions, all of your qualities as a golfer will be tested.
You have to send this message to all the players, and I'm going to do my best tomorrow.
We need to get away from our peers this time. Our mates also need to do their best."
In contrast to Bjorn, Jim Furik aims for the players he mentors to play more individually than the team, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have scored three out of four points.
Furik himself has been in both positions for the result of the final day of his destiny. The team he had previously belonged to was leading halfway at Brookline, but was ultimately defeated by the European team performing the "Miracle of Medina".
When asked about Ben Krenshaw's call for the final day, he replied, "I remember every word clearly."
“There will be twelve important matches tomorrow, but it will be expected to happen as quickly as we saw in Brookline and Medina.
When the momentum comes, there will be a lot of pressure in the middle.
We strategized according to the situation and selected people according to our intuition. Tomorrow we are trying to do a miracle."
Thomas is in charge of catching up, and in the top match, Rory McIlroy will compete. In addition, Paul Casey, Justin Rose, John Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood, and Ian Poulter will compete against the top-ranked European teams.
"We've placed players in this order because I think we're going to get through the whole thing well," Beyon explained, explaining the selection of singles.
Germany's new militance postponed
The German Navy had planned to renovate a Cold War-era warship, which is currently aging, into a state-of-the-art frigate in 2014, but due to system problems and ever-inflating costs, local media reported that it could not be realized, at least until next year.
According to Dee Zeit newspaper, which quoted a military spokesman's remark, the launch of "Rheinland Faltz," the lead ship of the Baden-Würtenberg-class frigate, was extended until the first half of 2019.
The battleships were expected to be introduced in 2014, but as troubles continued after delivery, and the ambitious plans went significantly crazy.
The Baden-Württemberg-class frigate, which was ordered by the Navy in 2007, is supposed to be used by the military in place of Bremen-class frigate ships, which are now dilapidated.
In addition to powerful cannons, anti-aircraft missiles and anti-ship missiles, the Baden-Württemberg-class frigates are characterized by some stealth technologies such as reduced radars, infrared signatures, and acoustic signatures.
Another important feature is that the maintenance cycle is long, and it is possible to dispatch the frigate to a distance of up to two years.
However, Dee Zeite notes that as plans continue to be delayed, the state-of-the-art warship, in which Germany is supposed to play an important role in showing its military power abroad, will become obsolete by the time it is actually started in use.
This troubling F125 frigate was big news when the German Navy officially refused to launch last year and sent it back to Broam Uunt Foss in Hamburg.
For the first time, the Navy returned a warship to a shipbuilding company after delivery.
It's unclear what's behind the returns, but German media cites information that multiple significant "software and structural deficiencies" have been seen that warships have become incompatible with combat missions.
The Baden-Württemberg-class frigates are piloted by about 120 sailors, half of those needed for the Bremen-class from a generation ago, so the flaws in the software have a particularly significant impact.
In addition, the Baden-Württemberg-class frigates are so heavy that their performance will be degraded, as well as the Navy's future upgrades will be limited.
This 7,000 tonne "Rheinland Falce" weighs twice as much as a ship of the same class used by Germany in World War II.
In addition to structural defects, the overall cost of the project, including crew training, has also emerged as a problem.
The overall cost of the project is said to have ballooned from the original twenty-two euros to thirty-six euros to the present.
The issue of the latest frigate ships has become very important, partly due to warnings that the German Navy’s forces are weakening recently.
Earlier this year, German National Denial Commission Chairman Hans Peter Bartelz acknowledged that the Navy had indeed “lost warships with enough capacity to be deployed.”
He said the issue is becoming more serious over time because the use of old warships has been halted and there are no alternative warships.
He also expressed his regret that the Baden-Württemberg-class frigates would not be introduced to the Navy at all.
National Trust explores bat mystery
A new study being conducted on grounds in Scotland's high-pland region aims to shed light on how bats use their topography as they prey.
The study is expected to provide new information about the behavior of these unique flying mammals and contribute to future conservation efforts.
The study, conducted by researchers and colleagues at the National Trust for Scotland, tracks species such as ordinary bats, soprano pipistrels, brown-dung-yeers, and doventons at Westerros' Inverwe Gardens.
A dedicated recorder is installed at the main points on the premises to track the activity of bats throughout the season.
NHS officials and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using portable detectors.
All recorded sounds are analysed by experts, revealing the frequency of the bat’s calls, as well as what species they’re doing.
Habitat maps and reports will be produced based on the analysis, and the behavior of bats will be drawn in detail according to the topography.
NTS’s Rob Duwar conservation advisor hopes that research will reveal where is the most important habitat for bats and how their habitats are used.
This information can help determine the benefits of habitat management tasks, such as how to install and protect forests for bats and related species.
Across Scotland and the United Kingdom, bat populations have declined significantly over the past hundred years.
Bats are at risk of extinction due to construction and development work and habitat reductions that affect the situation of persevering trees.
In addition to the chemical treatment of paper and building materials for insect removal, attacks from pet cats, as well as windmills and lighting are also a risk for bats.
In fact, bats aren't blind.
However, because bats are nocturnal, they have a habit of relying on hearing rather than vision during predatory activities.
Bats make full use of their developed reverberatory localization capabilities to pinpoint insects and recognize obstacles on flight routes.
NTS, which manages more than 76,000 hectares of assets in the country's land, has taken bats very seriously.
NTS has ten experts who conduct regular surveys, inspect bat dwelling trees, and sometimes protect bats.
NTS is also Scotland's first bat-only protected area on the sleeve estate of Dam Fries and Galloway, home to eight of Scotland's ten species of bat species.
David Thompson, who manages the estate, says the area is an ideal environment for bats.
“This is the best place for bats,” he said.
“There are old buildings here, there are a lot of old trees, and they are all part of the bat’s suitable habitat.
However, there are still a lot of things that bats don’t know, so activities here and elsewhere can help us understand what’s needed to breed bats.”
If a bat accidentally destroys even a single tree, he could kill up to four hundred female bats and young bats, which could lead to the extinction of local bats, so it’s important to check the bats before carrying out conservation work inside the land.
Bats are designated as protected animals, and it is illegal to kill, abuse, interfere, or cut down trees.
Elizabeth Ferrell, a member of the Scottish branch of the Bat Protection Trust, is seeking support from people in the region.
"There's still a lot to learn about bats in Scotland, and we really don't really know how bats are acting," he said.
Ronaldo denies rape lawsuit, lawyer prepares to sue self-inflicted
Cristiano Ronaldo has called his allegations of rape “false,” calling them “false” and using his name to “just want to promote themselves.”
Ronaldo's lawyers are preparing to sue the German weekly magazine Der Spiegel, which published the allegations.
The Portuguese player, who is active in Juventus forward, has been accused of raping an American woman named Kathryn Mayorga in a Las Vegas hotel room last year.
Ronaldo is also suspected of handing the woman thirty-sevendy-five thousand dollars and asking for a settlement to make sure this didn’t happen, Dea Spiegel reported Friday.
A few hours after this was reported, the 33-year-old Ronaldo, through a live video on Instagram, called the coverage "fake news" to 142 million followers.
“No, no, no, no, no.
“What’s reported today is fake news,” Ronaldo, who has won the Ballon d’Or five times in the past, told the camera.
“The magazine wants to use my name to promote itself.
It's a common occurrence.
Do you want to raise the profile of your own magazine by giving my name, that's their job.
“I’m fine, no problem at all,” he added with a smile.
According to Reuters, Ronaldo’s lawyers are preparing to sue Dea Spiegel over the allegations, criticizing the magazine, saying it was “unforgivable in terms of privacy.”
According to Christian Schultz, Ronaldo claims “probably one of the most serious human rights violations in recent years, as well as a suffocation to meet the severity of the infringement.”
It is alleged that the incident occurred in a suite at the Palms Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas in 2009.
According to documents in the District Court for Clark County, Nevada, Ronaldo and Majoruga went to Ronaldo’s room after meeting at a nightclub, where Ronaldo allegedly raped Mayolga’s anus.
Mayorga said that Ronaldo knelt down after the incident occurred and said that he was “99%” and “good people” who were poised to the bad part of the “1%”.
According to the document, Ronaldo admitted that he had a sexual relationship between the two, but that it was an agreement.
Mayorga also said he went to the police and took pictures of the injuries at the hospital, but he later felt the fear of retaliation and was uneasy about “opportunity,” so he agreed to a settlement outside the courtroom.
Thirty-four-year-old Mayorga is now calling for the settlement to be rescinded due to the continuing trauma of the alleged incident.
At the time of the incident, Ronaldo was about to move from Manchester United to Real Madrid, and this summer he is moving to Italy's strongman Juventus for one euro.
Brexit Britain 'permanently remorses' loss of auto industry
Business Minister Gregg Clark said Britain would "permanently regret" losing its position as the world's leading car manufacturer by Brexit.
He added that he was “concerned” that the U.K. Toyota would suspend production at its Burnaston plant if the U.K. leaves the EU without a deal.
Business Minister Clark said "an agreement is needed."
The Japanese automaker said that if a no-deal Brexit was reached, the delays at the border would result in a loss to the business.
At Toyota's Burnaston plant, "Auris" and "Avensis" are manufactured, and 90 percent of the 150,000 vehicles produced last year were exported to European Union countries outside the UK.
Marvin Cook, director of Toyota's Burnaston plant, said: "If the U.K. forcibly leaves the EU at the end of March, we will stop manufacturing at the plant."
Other automakers in the UK, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have also raised concerns about leaving the EU without a deal on cross-border transactions.
For example, BMW is shutting down its “mini” factory at Oxford for a month after Brexit.
At the heart of automakers’ concerns is supply chain risks arising during a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota's manufacturing line is operated based on "just-in-time," with custom-made vehicles using parts arriving every 37 minutes from suppliers in the UK and in the EU.
According to the automobile manufacturing industry, if the U.K. leaves the EU without a deal on March 29th, it could lead to delays at the border and delays or shortages of parts.
Toyota said it would not be possible to retain more than a day's worth of inventory at the company's Derbyshire plant, so manufacturing would be halted.
Clark said the "chequers plan" presented by Prime Minister Theresa May for future relations with the EU "has been elaborately crafted to evade checks at the border that the industry is concerned about."
“We need an agreement, which means we want to have the best agreement, not only to enjoy the current success, but also to take advantage of this event as an opportunity,” he said in a statement on BBC Radio 4Today’s programme.
“What not only Toyota but also other manufacturers have shown is that we need to be able to continue the supply chain, which has been functioning very well so far.”
Toyota said it was unclear how long it would take to stop manufacturing, but in the long run, additional costs would reduce the factory’s competitiveness and ultimately affect employment.
Peter Tsoubalaris, who has worked at the Bernaston plant for twenty-four years and is the president of the union association at the plant, said there are growing concerns among its members: "In terms of experience, there will never be a demand again if we lose this kind of work.
A spokeswoman for the government said: "The government has presented a credible and credible plan for the future relationship between the UK and the EU."
"Trump and Rosenstein's meeting could be re-postponed" White House
A high-level meeting between President Donald Trump and Deputy Attorney General Rob Rosenstein could be postponed "one more week" due to the ongoing dispute over Supreme Court Justice Brett Kavanaugh, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of Special Agent Robert Moler, who is conducting an investigation into Russia’s election meddling, Trump’s connection to Russia, and the president’s obstruction of justice.
There has been a lot of attention around Washington in recent months as to whether President Trump has replaced the deputy attorney general and will influence Mora’s independence.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Mr. Rosenstein attended the meeting wearing a device to record a conversation with President Trump, indicating the possibility of dismissing the president through the 25th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution.
Mr. Rosenstein denied the reports.
But last Monday he went to the White House and, according to media reports, signaled his resignation.
In response to this, it was announced that a meeting with President Trump, who was at the United Nations headquarters in New York at that time, will be held on Thursday.
President Trump said he “doesn’t want” Rosenstein’s replacement, but the meeting was later postponed due to a series of hearings and appointments by the Senate Judiciary’s Committee. At the hearing, Kavanaugh and Dr. Christine Brasey Ford, one of the women accused of indecent acts, testified.
On Friday, Mr. Trump ordered a one-week FBI investigation into Mr Kavanagh's accusations. Voting in the Senate would be further delayed.
Trump's spokeswoman, Sarah Saunders, spoke to Fox News on Saturday.
Asked about her meeting with Mr. Rosenstein, she replied: "That schedule isn't over yet, it could be this week. There's everything that's going to happen in the Supreme Court, so it wouldn't be surprising if it's postponed for a week.
It will become clear in the future, and I would like to convey the latest information in the press announcement at all times."
Some of the reporters will disagree on that remark: Sanders hasn't made a press briefing at the White House since September 10.
Chris Wallace, the host, asked why.
Saunders said he didn''t hate TV reporters' "stand-playing," but he also said: "There's no denying that they're stand-playing.
It suggests there will be more opportunities for direct contact between Trump and the press.
“President Trump is spending a lot of time on question-and-answer sessions compared to previous presidents,” he said, but he didn’t show any evidence: “I’ve actually checked the number of times.”
The briefing will continue to be held in the future, but Sanders said: "If there's a chance for the press to ask questions directly to the President of the United States, it should be absolutely better than asking me a question.
We’re making a lot of effort to make this happen. You know we’ve done a lot over the past few weeks, and if you can create an opportunity to talk to the President of the United States, it’s going to be an alternative to a press briefing,” he said.
Mr. Trump often accepts questions when he goes out from the White House to participate in public sessions and press conferences to welcome guests.
However, single press conferencing is rare.
The reason is self-evident due to the president’s nature, such as free-spirited behavior in New York this week and sometimes unnatural appearances in front of gathered reporters.
Insurance and Medical Service minister releases letter for workers from the EU at Scotland's NHS who are unsettled due to Brexit
The health care minister has thanked EU staff working for the NHS in Scotland as a member of the country and his desire to remain in the same position after Brexit.
Now that it is six months away from the EU, MSP's Jean Freema has published the letter.
The Scottish government has pledged to provide EU citizens who have already worked in the government's developed public services to meet the costs associated with their settlement applications.
In that letter, Freeman wrote: "During this summer, negotiations between the UK and the EU on the postponement of Brexit have been ongoing, and it is expected to reach a conclusion this fall.
However, the British government is preparing for it, taking into account the possibility of a no-deal scenario.
There is no doubt that it is a very restless time for all of you now.
Therefore, I would like to reiterate that I think that the contributions of all the staff, regardless of nationality, are very valuable.
Colleagues from EU and other countries bring valuable experiences and technologies, improve the functioning of health services, and contribute to the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is definitely your home, and I strongly hope that you will stay here.
Christion Abercromby, injured in the head, undergoes emergency surgery
Kristion Abacrombie, Tennessee Tiger's line-back, suffered a head injury and underwent emergency surgery on Saturday when he was defeated 31-27 by Vanderbilt Comodoas, according to Tenesian's Mike Organ.
According to Tennessee head coach Rod Reed, the injury allegedly happened just before half-time.
"He came to the sideline and fell there," Reid said.
Trainers and medical staff took oxygen inhalation measures on the sideline before placing Aberomby on the stretcher, and transported with a stretcher for a detailed examination.
The Tennessee spokesperson told WSMV's Chris Harris that Abercrombie underwent surgery at Vanderbilt Medical Center in Nashville, Tennessee.
Harris added, "We don't yet know the details about the type/severity of the injury," adding that Tennessee has begun an investigation into when he was injured.
This was his first season in Tennessee, after Aberonby's second-year red-shirt, after his transfer from Illinois.
On Saturday, he made a total of five tackles before leaving the game, resulting in a total of 18 tackles throughout the season.
Stamp duty paid by foreign purchasers of British real estate to increase
Foreign buyers will pay a higher stamp duty when buying real estate in the United Kingdom. The proceeds will be used to support the homeless under the newly-determined Tory Plan.
Mr Corbyn had succeeded in garnering youth votes, but the move is expected to counter this.
The stamp tax increase is aimed at people who do not pay taxes in the UK.
The Revenue Agency is expected to make £12 million in revenue per year, which will be used to support the homeless.
Prime Minister Theresa May announced today that foreign buyers will be subject to a higher stamp duty when buying property in the U.K., and the proceeds will be used to support the homeless.
Jeremy Corbyn has succeeded in garnering youth votes, but the move is seen as an attempt to counter their forces by promising to tax high-income groups while increasing the provision of cheap housing.
The stamp tax increase will be imposed on individuals and corporations who do not pay taxes in the UK. The proceeds will be used to strengthen the government's measures against people living on the streets.
The amount of the tax increase will be added to the current stamp duty, and it will be an increase of up to three percent. Two years ago, an increase was introduced for second homes and rental residences.
The Revenue Agency predicts that this will generate revenue of £12 million per year.
It is estimated that 13% of London’s new buildings are being purchased by residents outside the EU. This has led to soaring housing prices, making it increasingly difficult for first-time buyers to buy homes.
The UK’s wealthy regions, especially the Tokyo metropolitan area, have become “ghost towns” due to an increase in foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the UK.
The new policy was announced a week after Boris Johnson proposed a reduction in stamp duty to make it easier for young people to buy their first homes.
He accused major construction companies of occupying the land to keep housing prices high, urging Prime Minister Theresay to repeal the ceiling on the number of cheap dwellings in order to correct Britain’s "housing problem."
Corbyn announced a series of proposals that are attracting attention as housing reform, including curbing rents and eradicatining the pursuit of “disruption.”
He also wants to empower Congress more powerfully to build new homes.
Prime Minister Theresa May said: “Last year, I said that I will strive to rebuild the British Dream through the position of Prime Minister, and the lives of each new generation should be better.
It is also about restoring our country’s broken housing markets.
The UK is always open to people who want to live, work and build their lives here.
However, while British residents are working diligently, it is by no means favorable in a situation in which uninhabited individuals in the UK and foreign companies can easily purchase housing.
For many people who have dreams of owning a home, this has become a distant dream, and the disregard for the dignity of people living on the streets remains a very real problem.”
Jack Ross: "My ultimate dream is to be the manager of the Scottish team"
Sunderland's boss, Jack Ross, said his "ultimate dream" would one day become Scotland's team manager.
He is trying to reinstate the North East Club. He is currently in third place in League One, with a three-point difference from the top.
He led St. Milan to the Scottish Premiership last season and moved to the Stadium of Wright this summer.
“I wanted to play in my home country as a player.
“I got the B-cap, but that’s all,” Ross told BBC Scotland’s sporting sound.
“When I was a kid, I grew up looking at the Scottish team with my dad in Hamden, and it was something that always brought me back.
Opportunities like this can't be gained unless the club's management succeeds."
Prior to Mr. Ross, the managers of Sunderland were Dick Adfokart, David Moyes, Sam Aladice, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gustavo Poje and Paolo Di Carnio.
The former boss of Aloa Athletic is not afraid of being named celebrities at such a well-known club, he has been denied a transfer from Burnsley and Ipswich Town in the past.
“For me, success is determined by whether or not we can bring this club back to the Premier League.
“Because the organization and facilities of this club are, without a doubt, the Premier League itself,” he said.
"It's not going to be easy to get that far, but I don't think it's going to be a success unless we bring the club back there."
Mr. Ross is still three years in manager, but he is an assistant boss at Dumbarton and has fifteen months of experience as a coaching staff at Hearts.
He then reintroduced Aloa from downgrading him to third place, leading St. Milan, who had been downgraded, to the winner of the championship title next season.
Ross says he's more relaxed now than when he played for Clyde, Hartlepur, Volkerk, St Millan and Hamilton Academic.
"It was really a turning point in my life," he says, looking back at the moment when he took possession of Alore.
"I sincerely thought that management was better for me than the players.
I was able to do well as a player, so this might seem like a strange thing. I was earning a certain amount of money, and I made a certain amount of results.
But it's tough to play.
There’s a lot of work to be done every week.
In terms of stress and pressure, there are the same parts, but it seems that management is suitable for me.
I've always wanted to be involved in management, and now that's what it's happened. I've never been able to be like this since I was an adult."
