According to the survey, it was found that the best time for drinking was at 5pm on Saturday.
Pubs in the UK are suffering from financial difficulties, but their sales have been extended due to the intense heat of the summer. Meanwhile, for restaurant chains, this has become an additional burden.
According to the survey results, July's sales were up 2.7 percent at pub bars and 4.8 per cent in restaurants.
Peter Martin, a business consultant CGA who compiled the survey results, said: "It's been a long day of sunshine and we've been doing well at the World Cup for a longer time than expected in the U.K., and July was roughly the same as in June of the previous month. Pubs increased by 2.8 percent, but it was a further blow to the restaurant.
The restaurant business in June was down 1.8%, but July became even tougher.
Compared to the same period of the previous year, pubs and bars, with beverages as their main products, have performed the best. This is larger than the decline in restaurants.
The pub, whose main product is a meal, is not as good as the restaurant business, but it is tormented by the strength of the sunshine.
It seems that people are going out for a drink.
Sales of beverages in general-owned pubs and bars increased by 6.6 percent from the previous month, but by 3 per cent in terms of meals.”
Paul Newman, leisure and hospitality analyst at RSM, said: “These results continue the trends we have seen since the end of April.
Weather and large-scale social/sport events are factors that have a significant impact on the sales of the food service industry.
It is no longer surprising that the restaurant group's financial difficulties continue, but it will be particularly painful that sales have decreased by 4.8% compared to the same period of the previous fiscal year due to sustained cost pressure.
The lingering heat of the summer came at the worst possible time for businesses with their main meals. It will become clear in the future that the temperature has calmed down since the beginning of August will bring the long-awaited relief."
July's sales across pubs and restaurants, including new openings, increased by 2.7 percent, reflecting the stalling of the brand's development.
Kafer Peach Tracker monitors industrial sales in the pub, bar, and restaurant sectors in the U.K. and collects and analyzes performance data from forty-seven management groups. The total consolidated revenue is nine billion pounds, making it a well-known benchmark in the industrial sector.
