An advertisement for a milking machine that made the mother look like a dairy cow, two opinions online
The manufacturer of the milking machine has released an ad that looks like a dairy cow that is milking a nursing mother, and online opinion is divided into two.
Elby, a consumer technology company, released an advertisement inspired by a comical music video during the announcement of the world’s first silent wearable milking machine. The intention of the ad was to express the release of nursing mothers with this new milking machine.
The four real mothers danced in a straw-covered cowhouse with songs that included the following lyrics: "I'm milking by myself, but I don't think I'll have a tail, maybe I didn't notice it, but it's my breast, not the cow's breast."
The chorus continues: “Milk milk, milk it, give it to the baby to drink; milk the milk, milk the milk, and I milk the human woman.”
The ad, published on the company's Facebook page, sparked controversy online.
The video has been viewed 77,000 times and garnered hundreds of comments. The reaction from the audience has been controversial, many of which have exposed the "fear" of the dairy industry in broad daylight due to the ad.
“The use of dairy cows in the advertising of this product is a very wrong decision.
Just like us, dairy cows need to get pregnant and give birth to milk. The difference is that in the case of cows, they are separated from the baby a few days after giving birth," one of the posts wrote.
Elby's milking machine fits unobtrusively on the inside of a nursing bra (Elby/mother)
Other comments include: "Naturally, this will be traumatic for the mother and the baby.
“How about using a cow, even though a human mother can’t take away her baby?”
One added: "What an improbable advertisement."
There are some people who defend this ad, and a woman says, "I burst out laughing" at this song.
“I think it’s a genius idea.
If you’re still breastfeeding, you’ll still be using it yourself.
When I milk, I really feel like a cow.
I was convinced that the advertisement was just a little crazy.
“It’s a great product,” he said.
Others commented: "It's an interesting advertisement for moms who are breastfeeding (often at work or in the bathroom) and want to be a cow.
It's not an advertisement that praises or criticises the dairy industry."
At the end of the video, the women who were dancing were hiding their pumps in their bras.
The campaign’s concept is inspired by the fact that a lot of women who are breastfeeding say they look like cows.
However, since the Elvie Pump does not make any sound at all, without wires or tubes, and unobtrusively fits in a lactating bra, women can move freely, hold their baby, or even go out while breastfeeding.
Anna Ballalin, a partner and ECD partner of Mother, said: "Elvie Pump is an innovative product, so I think it was a bold and exciting sell-off.
By comparing female expressions with dairy cows, the aim was to attract attention to lactation and all its problems, and to show the amazingly free sensation of new lactation.
It's not the first time the Elvie Pump has been featured.
During London Fashion Week, the mother of two children using this product appeared on the runway with the designer Marta Jakubosky.
Hundreds of immigrant children secretly transferred them to a tent camp on the border of Texas.
Although the number of border crossings in a month was relatively unchanged, the number of migrant children who were detained has skyrocketed, partly due to severe rhetoric and policies by the Trump administration, which has made it difficult to entrust children to guarantors.
Until now, most of the guarantors are smuggled immigrants themselves, and they have been afraid of endangering their own stay by applying for children.
In June, the federal authorities announced that guarantors and other adults in their households would be required to file fingerprints, and that data would be shared with immigration authorities increased the risk.
Matthew Albens, a senior employee of the Immigration and Customs Enforcement Agency, testified in Congress that he had arrested dozens of unaccompanied minor guarantors.
The agency later confirmed that 70 percent of those arrested did not have a criminal record.
“Nearly 80 percent of guarantors or guarantor householders stay illegally in the country, most of whom are foreigners with criminal records.
That's why we're continuing to track down such individuals," Albens said.
According to the workers at the camp, in order to quickly process the child’s application, the authorities introduced a new rule that required some people to appear in court within a month, rather than 60 days after detention, which had previously been the standard so far.
Many of them will appear on video calls rather than their appearances and apply for a legal position with a immigration judge.
Those who are judged to be unqualified for relief will be deported immediately.
According to reports by workers and insiders in the camps in recent months, the prolonged detention of their children makes them prone to anxiety and depression, resulting in violence and escape.
Advocates said there was growing concern that the size of large facilities, such as Torniyo, showed signs that children were facing difficulties being overlooked.
He added that emotionally preparing and transferring children to tents without giving them enough time to say goodbye to their friends could exacerbate the trauma they have already suffered.
