India earthquake and tsunami
At least 384 people are expected to be killed and the death toll is expected to rise due to the strong earthquake and tsunami that hit Indonesia's Pal city on Friday, officials said.
Communication has been disrupted, and rescue officials have been unable to obtain information from Dongala province in the northern part of Pal, which is close to the epicenter of magnitude 7.5.
In Pal, 1 to 6 thousand people were evacuated after the disaster occurred.
Next, we show the main facts about the Pals and Dongala of Sulawesi.
Pal is the capital of the central Sulawesi state on the edge of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island, with an estimated population of 379,800 in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary at the time of the earthquake and tsunami.
Dongala is a province more than 300km along the northwestern coastline of Sulawesi.
The province is an administrative district smaller than the state, with an estimated population of 299,200 in 2017.
Fisheries and agriculture are the main industries of the economy of Central Sulawesi, particularly in the coastal areas of Dongara.
Nickel-mining is also important in the state, but it is largely concentrated in the Morowari on the coast on the other side of Sulawesi.
According to Indonesia's Disaster Management Agency, Pal and Dongara have been hit by tsunamis several times over the past 100 years.
In 1938, more than 200 people were killed by the tsunami, and hundreds of homes were destroyed in Dongara.
West Dongara was also hit by a tsunami in 1996, killing 9 people.
Indonesia is located in the Pacific Ring of Fire, with frequent earthquakes.
A recent large earthquake and a tsunami are shown below.
On December 26, 2004, a major earthquake struck on the west coast of Indonesia's Sumatra northern province of Aceh, and a tsunami affected by 14 countries resulted in 226,000 deaths along the Indian Ocean coastline, more than half of which were in Aceh state.
In 2005, a strong earthquake struck the west coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
Hundreds were killed on the island of Nias off the Sumatra coast.
2006: An earthquake of magnitude 6.8 occurred in the southern part of Indonesia's most populous island of Java, causing a tsunami hitting the southern coast, killing nearly 700 people.
In 2009, there was a magnitude 7.6 earthquake near Padang, the provincial capital of West Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people died.
2010: A magnitude 7.5 earthquake struck one island in the Mentawai Islands off the island of Sumatra, resulting in a tsunami of up to 10 metres, destroying dozens of villages, killing about 300 people.
2016: The shallow earthquake at the epicenter in Pidi Jaya, Aceh, caused devastating damage, causing people to panic in memory of the 2004 earthquake and tsunami.
There was no tsunami this time, but more than 100 people were killed by the collapse of the building.
2018: A major earthquake hit Indonesia's tourist island of Lombok, killing more than 500 people, mostly on the north side of the island.
Thousands of buildings were destroyed by the earthquake, and a large number of tourists were temporarily stranded.
