Earlier this year, U.S. President Donald Trump issued a warning by taxing major Chinese exports, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The largest tariffs that have progressed this week will affect items worth $200 billion (£150bn) due to new taxes, and will be taxed in virtually half of products imported from China into the U.S.
Beijing retaliated in the same way, most recently taking five to ten per cent taxes on $600 billion worth of U.S. products.
China has vowed to oppose the U.S. each time, and the world’s second-largest economy does not seem to withdraw anytime soon.
Succumbing to Washington would mean surrender, but openly following the U.S. is a great humiliation for China's supreme leader Xi Jinping.
Still, experts say that if Beijing handled the card correctly, by curbing the interdependencies of the two economies, the pressures of the U.S. trade war could positively support China in the long run.
"The fact that an early political decision by Washington or Beijing creates a state in which economic turmoil begins in both countries is more dangerous for bystanders than they had previously thought," Abigail Grace, a research associate at the New American Security Center at a think tank, said Abigail Grace, a research associate who focuses on Asia.
Syria's foreign minister 'ready' to return refugees
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and appeals for help in rebuilding a country devastated by a seven-year-long war.
According to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walido al-Muarem said the country's condition was improving.
“Today, thanks to the progress of the struggle against terrorism, the situation on the ground has become more stable and safer,” he said.
The government is continuing to repair areas that have been destroyed by terrorists toward the restoration of the normal.
All conditions are now being prepared for refugees who voluntarily return to their countries, having to flee due to terrorism, their daily lives, and their livelihood-biased economic measures.
The U.N. estimates that more than 550 million Syrians have been evacuated since the start of the war in 2011.
The other six million people are still living in the country and in need of humanitarian assistance.
Al-Muarem said the Syrian regime would welcome support for the reconstruction of a collapsed country.
However, he stressed that he would not accept conditional assistance or support from countries sponsoring civil unrest.
Europe wins Ryder's Cup in Paris
At Le Golf National on the outskirts of Paris, France, the U.S. team was stunned, and with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5, the European Union team won the 2018 Ryder Cup.
The U.S. has now recorded six consecutive defeats in fighting in Europe, and in Europe it hasn't won the Ryder Cup since 1993.
In Europe, Danish captain Thomas Björn's team reached 14.5 points needed to overthrow the United States and reclaimed the crown.
America's star Phil Mickelson, who had stalled in most of the tournament, dropped a tee shot into the pond on the par-three's 16th hole and admitted his match to Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari has shone in all of his rounds, making him one player out of four, aiming for 5-0-0, the current format of the tournament, which began in 1979.
The U.S.'s Jordan Spieth lost five and four to Denmark's Thorbyon Olsen, the lowest-ranked player of the European team.
The world's top-ranked player, Dustin Johnson, dropped two and one to England's Ian Poulter, who would have played in the final game of the Ryder Cup.
Sergio García, a veteran of the eight-time Ryder Cup, became Europe's biggest winner of the tournament with 25.5 career points.
“I don’t usually cry, but I don’t have anything to do today.
It's been a very difficult year.
I am so grateful that Thomas chose and believed in me.
I am very happy. I'm really happy to get the cup back.
It's a team, and I'm very happy to be able to contribute."
He passed the baton to John Lamb, a fellow of his hometown, defeating American golf legend Tiger Woods in Sunday's singles with two and one.
"It's an incredible amount of pride to beat Tiger Woods, I grew up looking at him," the 23-year-old Ram said.
Woods lost all of his matches in France in the fore and recorded a 13-21-3 career on the Ryder Cup record.
Jack Nicklaus is the only strange statistic by an unprecedented, talented player who wins fourteen major titles.
With the exception of high-quality plays during the tournament, the U.S. team had trouble looking for a fairway over the weekend, with the exception of Justin Thomas and Tony Finau.
American captain Jim Frükke spoke after his team's disappointing performance: "I'm proud of the team. They fought.
We discussed Europe this morning.
We had a fight.
Let's salute Thomas.
He's a great captain.
All twelve of his players played well.
We’re going to form a team again, working together with America’s PGA and the Ryder Cup Committee, to move forward.
I like these twelves very much, and I'm very proud to work as a captain.
You don’t have to throw away your hat.
We were defeated."
Red tide latest information: Decreased concentrations in pinellas, manatees, and Sarasota
Recent reports from the Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission have shown that red tide concentrations have averaged declines in parts of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, in the Pineras, Manatees, Sarasota, Charlotte, and Collier groups, the color status of the patcher has been reported, which indicates a decrease in concentrations.
The occurrence of red tides extends from the northern part of Pineras County to the 130-mile stretch of the southern coast of Lee County.
Patches can be found about 10 miles off the coast of Hillsborough County, but they can be found in few places compared to last week.
The red tide has also been observed in Pasco County.
Last week, medium concentrations were reported in Pineras County or off the coast, high concentrations were reported off the coast of Hillsboro County, high concentrations from element confirmation in Manatie County, high concentrations from element confirmation in Sarasota County or off the coast, high concentrations from element confirmation in Charlotte County, high concentrations from element confirmations in Lee counties or offshore, and low concentrations in Korean counties.
Respiratory inflammation continues to be reported in Pineras, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Korea counties.
There have been no reports of respiratory inflammation in northwest Florida this week.
